---
import VisionFigure from '../VisionFigure.astro';
import SectionHead from '../SectionHead.astro';
---

<section data-vision-section class="band-ink section">
  <SectionHead heading="Maryland, after." />

  <div class="container-page">
    <VisionFigure num="7" title="The same arterial, before and after">
      <div class="street-ba">
        <div class="street-panel street-panel-now">
          <span class="street-panel-label">Today</span>
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 300 100"
            preserveAspectRatio="none"
            role="img"
            aria-label="Today: a six-lane arterial posted at 45 mph, with two narrow sidewalks"
          >
            <rect class="st-sidewalk" x="0" y="40" width="20" height="40"></rect>
            <rect class="st-car" x="20" y="40" width="40" height="40"></rect>
            <rect class="st-car" x="60" y="40" width="40" height="40"></rect>
            <rect class="st-car" x="100" y="40" width="40" height="40"></rect>
            <rect class="st-car" x="140" y="40" width="40" height="40"></rect>
            <rect class="st-car" x="180" y="40" width="40" height="40"></rect>
            <rect class="st-car" x="220" y="40" width="40" height="40"></rect>
            <rect class="st-car" x="260" y="40" width="20" height="40"></rect>
            <rect class="st-sidewalk" x="280" y="40" width="20" height="40"></rect>
            <text class="st-label" x="150" y="32" text-anchor="middle">6 lanes · 45 mph</text>
          </svg>
        </div>
        <div class="street-panel street-panel-won">
          <span class="street-panel-label">Won</span>
          <svg
            viewBox="0 0 300 100"
            preserveAspectRatio="none"
            role="img"
            aria-label="Won: same width, redesigned with two bus lanes, two car lanes, two protected bike lanes, wider sidewalks, posted 25 to 35 mph"
          >
            <rect class="st-sidewalk" x="0" y="40" width="32" height="40"></rect>
            <rect class="st-bike" x="32" y="40" width="20" height="40" opacity="0.85"></rect>
            <rect class="st-buffer" x="52" y="40" width="8" height="40"></rect>
            <rect class="st-bus" x="60" y="40" width="40" height="40"></rect>
            <rect class="st-car" x="100" y="40" width="40" height="40"></rect>
            <rect class="st-car" x="140" y="40" width="40" height="40"></rect>
            <rect class="st-bus" x="180" y="40" width="40" height="40"></rect>
            <rect class="st-buffer" x="220" y="40" width="8" height="40"></rect>
            <rect class="st-bike" x="228" y="40" width="20" height="40" opacity="0.85"></rect>
            <rect class="st-sidewalk" x="248" y="40" width="32" height="40"></rect>
            <text class="st-label" x="150" y="32" text-anchor="middle"
              >2 bus · 2 car · 2 bike · 25–35 mph</text
            >
          </svg>
        </div>
      </div>
      <Fragment slot="caption">
        The street width doesn't change. What's on it does. Bus lanes carry more people in less
        space. Protected bike lanes save lives. Narrower drive lanes slow cars to speeds people
        survive.
      </Fragment>
    </VisionFigure>
  </div>

  <div class="container-narrow">
    <div class="vision-prose">
      <p>
        Las&nbsp;Vegas Boulevard, Maryland Parkway, Charleston, Sahara, Tropicana, Boulder Highway —
        the Valley's major arterials are six to eight lanes wide and posted for 45 miles per hour.
        They were built that way on purpose. The traffic engineering profession of the 1960s and 70s
        designed these streets to move cars quickly between developments, at the cost of every other
        use. People walking and biking were treated as failures of the model. They are still treated
        that way. In Clark County, pedestrians and cyclists die far out of proportion to their share
        of trips.
      </p>
      <p>
        The won Valley keeps these streets exactly as wide — and puts more on them. A six-lane
        arterial becomes a four-lane arterial with two dedicated bus lanes, two protected bike
        lanes, wider sidewalks with shade trees, and crosswalks every four hundred feet instead of
        every quarter-mile. Posted speeds drop to a range that humans can survive being hit at — 25
        to 35 in town, with 45-plus reserved for actual highways. Drivers complain about the extra
        minute on their commute. They get used to it. The deaths fall.
      </p>
      <p>
        New York, Denver, Stockholm, and Oslo have all run this redesign on streets just as wide as
        Maryland — and kept it. The engineering already exists. The political fight is the part we
        own.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .street-ba {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 1.5rem;
  }
  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .street-ba {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 2rem;
    }
  }
  .street-panel {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }
  .street-panel-label {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    font-weight: 700;
  }
  .street-panel-now .street-panel-label {
    color: var(--vp-text-muted);
  }
  .street-panel-won .street-panel-label {
    color: var(--color-primary);
  }
  .street-panel svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
  .street-panel svg .st-label {
    font-size: 9px;
    font-weight: 600;
    fill: currentColor;
  }
  .st-car {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 14%, transparent);
    stroke: currentColor;
    stroke-width: 0.5;
  }
  .st-bus {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-primary) 15%, transparent);
    stroke: var(--color-primary);
    stroke-width: 1;
  }
  .st-bike {
    fill: var(--color-primary);
  }
  .st-sidewalk {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 8%, transparent);
  }
  .st-buffer {
    fill: color-mix(in srgb, currentColor 30%, transparent);
  }
</style>
